"""Class 10 SST Ch 17 Outcomes of Democracy - extras."""
from common import AR4, Q

AR = [
 AR4(r"A democratic government is a legitimate government.", r"It is people's own government, and people have the right to choose it through elections.", "a",
     r"A government elected by the people and accepted as their own is legitimate, so R explains A."),
 AR4(r"Democracies are expected to produce a government that is accountable to the citizens.", r"The Right to Information Act allows citizens to ask for information from the government.", "b",
     r"Both are true; RTI is one means of accountability but does not by itself explain the principle."),
 AR4(r"A democratic government is expected to be transparent.", r"In a democracy citizens have the right to know about the decisions taken and the procedure followed, so that they can hold the government accountable.", "a",
     r"Open procedures allow citizens to examine decisions and check misuse; R explains A."),
 AR4(r"Democracy is considered better than other forms of government because it allows room to correct mistakes.", r"Democracies in the world have generally had slightly lower growth than dictatorships.", "b",
     r"Both are true (growth data of 1950-2000), but the growth figure is not the reason democracy allows mistakes to be corrected."),
 AR4(r"Democracy has completely removed economic inequalities in all countries.", r"In many democracies a small section of the population enjoys a very large share of the national income.", "d",
     r"A is false because inequalities persist; R is true."),
 AR4(r"Democracy promotes equality and dignity of citizens.", r"In a democracy the right to vote is given to a person according to wealth and education.", "c",
     r"A is true. R is false: universal adult franchise gives every adult citizen one vote irrespective of wealth or education."),
]

CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> A village has no proper road, and for years people complained to the local office. A villager then filed an application under the Right to Information Act asking how much money was sanctioned for the road and how it was spent. The office had to reply within a time limit, and the work was soon started. "
   r"(i) What did the villager use the Right to Information Act for? (ii) Which feature of democratic government does this show? (iii) Name the kind of government that is answerable to its citizens. (iv) How does such a right make government accountable?",
   r"(i) To obtain information on the money sanctioned and spent on the road (1). (ii) Transparency (1). (iii) An accountable government (1). (iv) Citizens can examine decisions and expenses and ask officials to explain; the fear of exposure makes the government careful about misuse and corruption (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> A teacher shows her class data on economic growth between 1950 and 2000. All dictatorial regimes had an average growth of 4.42 per cent a year and all democratic regimes 3.95 per cent. For poor countries the figures were 4.34 per cent under dictatorship and 4.28 per cent under democracy. "
   r"(i) Which type of regime had the higher average growth rate overall? (ii) By how much did it exceed the other overall? (iii) What was the difference among poor countries? (iv) Why can't this data prove that dictatorship is better than democracy?",
   r"(i) Dictatorial regimes (1). (ii) 0.47 percentage points (1). (iii) 0.06 percentage points, which is virtually no difference (1). (iv) The difference is small and growth is only one outcome; democracy also gives accountability, rights, dignity and the chance to correct mistakes, so growth alone cannot decide which is better (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In a country, a few people hold most of the wealth, while poor people live in difficult conditions. The poorest 20 per cent share only a small part of the national income, while the richest 20 per cent hold a very large share. Yet all adults have one vote each in elections. "
   r"(i) What does 'one person, one vote' show about the political position of people? (ii) Is there economic equality in this country? (iii) Which form of equality exists and which one does not? (iv) Why do we say that democracy has not yet removed economic inequalities?",
   r"(i) That all citizens are politically equal (1). (ii) No (1). (iii) Political equality exists; economic equality does not (1). (iv) Political equality has not automatically led to an equal share of income and assets, since the rich can influence decisions; the poor may still lack basic needs, so democracy alone is not enough (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

